QA correction for the step-4 amendment. Re-filed from chat (msg-2250d725, 20:39:12Z) - third EMPTY routing receipt on my sends tonight, so this is the durable copy.

THE MULTI-BEARING INVARIANT IS NOT ENFORCED IDENTICALLY AT TWO LAYERS. The core guard carries a leading clause that was dropped when it was cited as corroboration:

  planning-run-submission.ts:158-160
  if (state.runKind !== "EXPANSION" && bearing.length > 1) {
    return unsupported("MULTI_NODE_EXECUTION_UNSUPPORTED", bearing);
  }

Core EXEMPTS EXPANSION runs - a multi-bearing EXPANSION run finalizes cleanly. The dispatch-lane guard exempts nothing:

  foundation-attempt-contracts.ts:161  if (bearing.length > 1) return refuseLocal("FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_MULTI_NODE_UNSUPPORTED");
  grep -n EXPANSION apps/daemon/src/work/foundation-attempt-contracts.ts  ->  ZERO hits

So core refuses a strict SUBSET of what the daemon refuses. Two consequences for step 4 under (B)-sharpened:

(i) COPY THE DISPATCH RULE, UNCONDITIONAL - not core's. The re-verification runs on the foundation.dispatch route where admitSingleExecutionNode has already refused multi-bearing regardless of runKind. Importing core's EXPANSION exemption would put the new check in disagreement with the guard that ran 26 lines earlier (foundation-attempt-service.ts:192 vs :218).

(ii) THE REFUSAL ARM IS UNREACHABLE THROUGH PRODUCTION, and that dictates the drill. admitSingleExecutionNode at :192 runs strictly before the activation at :218, so any multi-bearing graph is refused before the derivation is called. A multi-bearing refusal test driven end-to-end is answered by the EARLIER guard - it passes with the new check deleted, which is the classic vacuous refusal test. The refusal must be exercised at the seam with a directly-constructed input, and the mutation drill must kill the NAMED new code, not the upstream guard. Defence-in-depth is worth having here; it simply cannot be graded end-to-end, and a test claiming it will be rejected.

OPEN QUESTION, not asserted, raised to governor-7211ec87: can an EXPANSION run reach foundation.dispatch? If yes, core permits a multi-bearing EXPANSION finalize that dispatch then refuses with FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_MULTI_NODE_UNSUPPORTED - a product defect rather than a layering choice, and worth its own row since the divergence is written down nowhere. If EXPANSION never reaches that route, the exemption is harmless and it is a documentation gap.
